      "footprint": [26, 22],
      "origin": [7, 5],
      "author": "Hollowmere team",
      "story": "Emeka Okafor farmed hay and milked a dozen cows at the head of Larch Hollow for forty years. His daughter Ife was home when the roads closed; her brother Chidi never made it back. Tom Merrow drove up from Pell's Crossing with a truckload of plywood and helped them board the front of the house. In the second week something got in among the cows, and Papa went out with the shotgun and came back holding his arm. Ife put him to bed downstairs and, when he stopped answering to his name, nailed his door shut. She carried the preserves, the potatoes and the last of the flour down to the root cellar, padlocked the cold room with Papa's strongbox inside, put a bolt on the pantry over the cellar hatch and hid the key where Papa kept his shotgun shells. Then six days of rain brought the bathroom down into the parlour, and Papa found the soft boards under his bed. Ife's journal ends upstairs. Tom Merrow walked home.",
